## Before you start

Have these to hand:

- your **National Insurance number**
- your **bank or building society details**
- your **GP's name, address and phone number**, and those of any other health professionals you see
- **dates** of any stays in hospital or a care home
- **dates** of any time spent abroad for more than 4 weeks

## Step 1: Start your claim

You can start your claim **by phone** or, in many areas, **online** at GOV.UK. Someone else can call for you, but you will need to be with them.

{% callout type="info" title="PIP new claims line" %}
- **Telephone:** 0800 917 2222
- **Textphone:** 0800 917 7777
- **Relay UK:** 18001 then 0800 917 2222

Monday to Friday, 8am to 6pm. Calls are free. If you cannot use the phone, you can ask for a paper form by writing to the DWP.
{% /callout %}

If you are terminally ill, say so. Your claim will be handled under the **special rules**, which are faster, and you will not need to fill in the long form.

## Step 2: Fill in the "How your disability affects you" form

{% callout type="good" title="Free printable guide" %}
Download our [question by question guide to the PIP form (PDF, 25 pages)](/guides/how-to-fill-in-your-pip-form.pdf). It explains every question, what scores points and what evidence helps, with example answers, a symptom diary and a checklist to use before you send the form.
{% /callout %}

The DWP will post you a form (called the PIP2), or you may be able to fill it in online. You normally have **one month** to send it back.

This form is the most important part of your claim. Tips:

- **Read the questions first.** They follow the 12 PIP activities. Our [self-check](/benefits/pip/points-checker/) can help you plan your answers.
- **Describe your typical and bad days,** not just your good days.
- **Explain the reliability rules for you.** Can you do it safely? Well enough? As often as you need? In a reasonable time? What happens afterwards?
- **Give real examples,** like "I fell getting out of the bath in March" or "I left the hob on twice last month".
- **Mention every aid you use,** or need but do not have.
- **Use the extra space.** Add pages if you need to. Put your name and National Insurance number on each one.
- **Do not leave boxes blank** unless they really do not apply.
- **Keep a copy** of the form and everything you send.

## Step 3: Send evidence

Evidence helps the DWP understand your needs. Useful evidence includes:

- letters or reports from your **GP, consultant, nurse, physiotherapist or occupational therapist**
- your **list of medicines** (a repeat prescription list is fine)
- a **symptom diary** covering a few weeks
- a short statement from a **family member, friend or carer** about the help you need
- care plans or support plans

Send copies, not originals. Do not delay sending the form to wait for evidence. You can send more later.

## Step 4: The assessment

Most people are asked to have an **assessment** with a health professional. It is usually by phone or video, or sometimes face to face. Read [how to prepare for your PIP assessment](/benefits/pip/assessment/).

## Step 5: The decision

A DWP decision maker looks at your form, your evidence and the assessment report, then sends you a **decision letter**. It tells you:

- whether you have been awarded PIP
- which parts and rates
- how many points you scored for each activity
- how long the award lasts

If you disagree, you have **one month** to ask the DWP to look again. See [how to challenge a PIP decision](/benefits/pip/challenge-a-decision/).

## Getting paid

PIP is usually paid **every 4 weeks** into your bank account. It is paid from the date you started your claim, so the first payment may include a back payment.

## Free help with your claim

- **Citizens Advice:** help with forms and appeals
- **Local welfare rights services:** often run by your council
- **Turn2us:** a free benefits calculator and grant search
- **Charities for your condition,** such as Fibromyalgia Action UK
